			 --------------------
			 O88 BUG LIST HISTORY
			 --------------------

Revision	Date		Product		
--------	----		-------		

3.01		1/1/88		O88.EXE

	Repaired problem with +J that caused O88 to crash on very
	large files.  Completely reworked in-line code expansion and
	absorption of ADD SI,imm instructions.  Repaired several large
	case bugs.


3.02		1/12/88		O88.EXE

	Repaired treatment of STOSB/W instruction, generated by
	in-line expansion of small case _setmem() function call.


3.03		1/18/88		O88.EXE

	Corrected a bug with register substitution when it straddles
	an inline expansion (+E, +M) of a library function.
	Corrected bugs in combining instructions that are separated
	by other instructions.  Corrected large case bugs with regard
	to what registers are used to return pointers (i.e., ES:SI
	instead of ES:BX).  O88 modified to output DSEG before ESEG
	for large case.  Repaired bug that caused END statement to
	appear before CSEG when there was only data and no code.
	Fixed bug that caused ESEG RB's, DB's, and DW's to be lost.
	Reworked the treatment of data and PUBLICs in DSEG and ESEG.


1.02		11/1/87		CC

    (a) Corrected problem with where C88 places the .A file needed
        by O88.  C88 version 2.6 places the .A file in the same
	directory as the .C file, while C88 version 3.0 places the
	.A file in the same directory as the .O file.  CC no longer
	issues the -O option to C88 when asking it to generate the
	.A file.

    (b) Repaired I/O redirection so that compilation with output
	of CC redirected to a file will now capture the O88 banner
	and status report in the file instead of putting them on the
	screen.  This does NOT change the way stand-alone operation
	of O88 is used.

	NOTE: This new version 1.02 of CC now requires version
	      3.04 or later of O88!


3.05		3/7/87		O88

	Repaired problem handling JMP to a label where the label
	was on the very next line of code.  The jump and the label
	were (correctly) removed as unnecessary, but the JMP turned
	on the "dead code" flag for all that followed the label.
	Also corrected bug with comparisoms of labels and jump 
	destinations.

3.05a		6/23/88		O88
1.02a		6/23/88		CC

	Modified O88 to return DOS termination code of 255 on error.
	Modified CC to continue if DOS termination code of C88, O88,
	or ASM88 is 1 (Warning) or 0 (Success), and to abort the job
	only for DOS termination codes of 2 or higher (Error).

3.05b		7/6/88		O88

	3.05a mods took care of serious errors, such as file i/o or
	memory allocation, but did not treat optimization errors,
	such as stack cleanup conflicts or undefined registers.  Now
	O88 returns a DOS termination code of 255 for all error
	conditions.  Note, however, that O88 continues to run in
	the presence of optimization errors, but that in such cases,
	CC will terminate the compile after O88 without running ASM88.

1.03		7/18/88		CC

	Added support for 'M' (DOS LINK) option of C88 & ASM88.
	Requires that TOOBJ.EXE, C.OBJ, C88.LIB from the DOS LINK 
	option package be in the current directory or path.

3.06		8/10/88		O88
1.04		8/10/88		CC

	Edited the declaration of printf() and fclose() in the
	*.C files on the distribution disk to be compatible with
	the recently introduced declarations in the STDIO.H file
	of the version 3.xx compiler package.

	CC has been modified to now allow the slash ('/') character 
	that separates C88 and O88 command line arguments to be placed
	more freely:

		CC file c88args / o88args	(worked before)
		CC file c88args /o88args	(worked before)
		CC file c88args/o88args		(now works)
		CC file c88args/ o88args	(now works)

	In-line #asm in a switch statement sometimes caused O88 to
	forget that a label was needed, and it subsequently deleted
	it, causing assembly errors. (Repaired.) 

	When REP STOSB was replaced by REP STOSW (with CX replaced
	by CX/2, O88 did not copy AL to AH. (Repaired.)

	FSTSW and FISTP instructions inserted by +7 option modify their
	destination arguments, but this semantic was overlooked by O88
	in the code where it keeps track of register contents.  (Repaired.)

	Functions called with lots of words (~15 or more) pushed onto
	the stack (as arguments) caused O88 to spend a pseudo-infinite
	time in a two-dimensional recursion. (Repaired:  This processing
	was only needed in case a +E or +M expansion generated PUSH-POP
	pairs that might be replaced by MOV's.  Since the worst case
	in-line expansion generates 5 PUSH-POP pairings, I simply limited
	the corresponding depth of the recursion.  'Also did a pre-check
	before beginning the recursion to see if the PUSH's were even
	followed by an expanded CALL - if not, the PUSH-POP processing
	is avoided.  Besides fixing the bug, this speeds up O88 by
	eliminating a lot of unnecessary processing.)

	Added error detection on +E and +M in case user calls any of the
	corresponding library functions with the wrong number of arguments.


3.06a		8/19/88		O88

	The +7 in-line expansion of calls to library function _FCMP and
	_FCMPKEEP contained bad code.  (Repaired.)

	Replacement of:

			XOR AX,AX
			CWD
			MOV WORD _FTEMP,AX
			MOV WORD _FTEMP[2],DX
			FILD DWORD _FTEMP

	by FLDZ did not require the XOR to use AX.  Repaired.


3.06b		8/28/88		O88

	MOV DS,WORD xyz was being simplified to MOV DS,ES if
	ES contained WORD xyz.  (Repaired.)

3.06c		8/30/88		O88

	The following code optimizations were incorrectly applied:

		MOV BX,DS			#
		ADD BX,4096		==>	ADD DS,4096
		MOV WORD nseg_,BX		#

		MOV AX,SP			#
		ADD AX,512		==>	ADD SP,512
		MOV WORD mem,AX			MOV WORD mem,SP

	(Repaired.)

	XOR AX,AX (or MOV AX,0) followed by MUL (or IMUL) was
	being replaced by bad code.  (Repaired.)

	The following optimization:

		MOV r1,src1		MOV r2,src1
		opc dst2,src2	=>	opc dst2,src2
		SHL r1,imm		SHL r2,imm
		... (more SHL's)	... (more SHL's)
		MOV r2,r1

	didn't check that the register in the SHL instructions
	was the same as that in the two MOVs.  (Repaired.)

3.06d		2/7/89		O88

	In-line code generated for _peekw, _peekd, _pokew, _poked
	was incorrect.  Repaired.


